===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|envirounen}}, {{m|enm|enviroun|t=to surround in a circle or ring, or on the perimeter; to beset, besiege; to cover, enclose, envelop; to provide a setting or surrounding to; to move in a circle; to move around the perimeter; to go, move, or wander about (a place); to fill or pervade (a place); to run all the way through}}{{nb...|enueroun, enuerown, enueyron, enuiron, enuyron, enuyronne, enuyroun, enuyrounne, enuyrowne, enverom, enveron, enveroun, enverounen, enverroun, environ, environne, enviroune, envirowne, envyron, envyrone, envyroun, envyroune, envyrown|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=envīrǒunen|pos=v|id=MED14191}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|envirouner}}{{nb...|enviruner, envyronner|otherforms=1}}, {{der|en|frm|environner}}, and {{der|en|fro|environner|t=to arrange in a circle; to circumnavigate, travel around; to traverse, wander around; to encircle, encompass, surround}}{{nb...|enviruner|otherforms=1}} (modern {{cog|fr|environner}}), from {{m|fro|environ|pos=adverb}} (see [[#Etymology 1|etymology 1]])<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=63080|date=September 2022|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref> + {{m|fro|-er|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming {{glossary|verb}}s}}.

{{rel-top|cognates}}
* {{cog|ca|environar}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}
* {{cog|fro|avironer}}, {{m|fro|avironner}} ({{cog|frm|avironer}}, {{m|frm|avironner}}, {{cog|xno|avironer}}, {{m|xno|aviruner}})
* {{cog|es|environar}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}
{{rel-bottom}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To [[encircle]], to [[surround#Verb|surround]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Titus Andronicus Q1|act=III|scene=i|page=51|passage=For now I ſtand as one vpon a rocke, / '''Inuirond''' with a wildernes of ſea, / VVho markes the waxing tide, grow waue by waue, / Expecting euer when ſome enuious ſurge, / VVill in his briniſh bowels ſwallow him.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=III|canto=V|stanza=39|passage=Dwelling in a pleasant glade, / With mountains round about '''environed'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-3|act=II|scene=i|passage='''Environed''' he was with many foes.}}
#* '''a. 1631''', {{w|John Donne}}, ''Elegy XII: His Parting from Her''
#*: '''Environ''' me with darkness whilst I write.
#* '''1673''', [[w:John Milton|John Milton]], “[[s:I did but prompt the age to quit their cloggs|I did but prompt the age to quit their cloggs]]”:
#*: I did but prompt the age to quit their cloggs
#*: By the known rules of antient libertie,
#*: When strait a barbarous noise '''environs''' me
#*: Of Owles and Cuckoes, Asses, Apes and Doggs.
#* {{RQ:Charlotte Bronte Jane Eyre|volume=II|chapter=X|page=268|passage=On sleeping, I continued in dreams the idea of a dark and gusty night. {{...}} [T]otal obscurity '''environed''' me; rain pelted me; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Haunted House|page=1|column=1|chapter=The Mortals in the House|passage=Under none of the accredited ghostly circumstances, and '''environed''' by none of the conventional ghostly surroundings, did I first make acquaintance with the house which is the subject of this Christmas piece.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1894|author=Henry Gough|author2=James Parker|title=A Glossary of Terms Used in Heraldry|page=231
|text=Azure, an annulet '''environing''' a barrulet, between two bars, and in chief &amp; cross patty fitchy {{...}} }}

